WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.967
D V C, data version control.

2
00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:07.800
It gives git-like versioning to multi-gigabyte datasets and model files.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.867
Here is the elegant mechanism.

4
00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:13.300
You run dvc add on a big file.

5
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.033
DVC hashes it.

6
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.967
The bytes move to a remote store, like S3.

7
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:21.376
And a tiny pointer file with the hash stays behind.

8
00:00:21.376 --> 00:00:22.900
That is what git commits.

9
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.267
Now watch history.

10
00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:28.367
Two commits with unchanged data share one blob.

11
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:32.659
The third commit changes the data, so it points at a new hash.

12
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:36.200
Check out any commit, and DVC restores its exact bytes.

13
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.833
Here it is in a terminal.

14
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.033
We add the dataset, and the bytes go to the remote.

15
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:44.200
Git commits only the pointer file.

16
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.300
Later, we check out the older pointer, run dvc checkout, and the version one bytes are back.

17
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.233
DVC has a second act: pipelines.

18
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:59.533
A dvc yaml file declares stages with their dependencies and outputs.

19
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:04.906
Then dvc repro re-runs only the stages whose inputs actually changed.

20
00:01:04.906 --> 00:01:08.067
It is make for ML, with data-aware caching.

21
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:11.167
Remember the registry postmortem?

22
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.667
It asked which dataset trained production.

23
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:17.733
The tagged release commit holds a pointer with one specific hash.

24
00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:24.151
Check it out, run dvc checkout, and you hold byte-identical training data from months ago.

25
00:01:24.151 --> 00:01:26.667
Reproducibility becomes two commands.

26
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:28.736
Let's recap.

27
00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:31.200
DVC versions what git cannot hold.

28
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.867
Git commits a tiny pointer, and the bytes live in a remote.

29
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.800
Code and data travel together through history.

30
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.410
And dvc repro re-runs only what changed.

31
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.900
Next up: Kubeflow.
